 "id": "2010-sallis-physical",
 "name": "Physical Activity Neighborhood Environment Scale (PANES, 17-item questionnaire)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of PANES, the brief environmental module of the International Physical Activity Prevalence Study (IPS; self-administered version, revised November 2002). It was used with the IPAQ in 11 countries (Sallis et al. 2009, American Journal of Preventive Medicine; 11,541 adults) and evaluated for reliability against the longer NEWS-A by Sallis, Kerr, Carlson, Norman, Saelens, Durant & Ainsworth (2010, Journal of Physical Activity and Health 7, 533–540; 291 adults in 3 US cities). Participants think of the area all around their home that they could walk to in 10-15 minutes and answer 17 single items: main type of housing; then statements on shops, transit stop, sidewalks, bicycle facilities, recreation facilities, crime at night (core, items 1–7); traffic, seeing active people, interesting things to look at, number of motor vehicles (recommended, 8–11); four-way intersections, sidewalk and bicycle path maintenance, traffic for cycling, crime by day, places to go (optional, 12–17), on a 4-point strongly disagree–strongly agree scale with \"Don't know/Not sure\" and, where printed, \"Does not apply\" options. Item wording, answer labels and codes (77, 88) are taken word for word from the survey on the authors' website (drjimsallis.com). The page also stores the 0–6 activity-friendly index of the 11-country study (authors' scoring guide, approach 1). No images are needed. Differences from the original: (1) answers are tapped on screen, and the introduction for national coordinators and the translation guidelines are left out; (2) researchers who want only the core items can delete the later pages; (3) age and gender questions and the closing feedback are added by us. The authors ask that item wording and response scales are not changed, so translations should follow their guidelines. The template is taken down if they object.",
 "author": "Sallis, Kerr, Carlson, Norman, Saelens, Durant & Ainsworth",
 "year": "2010",
 "category": "Academic Research",
